{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Why do you ask for my name?</b> We have no fixed name, for our names change according to the mission." "<b>Or</b> he may say, 'Why do you ask for my name?' — it is not our way to reveal it, for no angel wishes to reveal his name so as not to appropriate crown and glory to himself, so that people would say, 'So-and-so performed this sign,' as though the matter depended on him; rather he is but a servant carrying out his mission. And this is what the angel said to Manoah (Judges 13:18): 'Why do you ask for my name, seeing it is פלאי (pelei)?' — that is, hidden: we do not wish to reveal it, so as not to appropriate crown and glory, for the glory belongs to none but the Holy One, blessed be He, in the manner of what is said (Isaiah 43:7): ״כל הנקרא בשמי ולכבודי בראתיו״, and it is written (Psalms 29 — the citation has כ״ט = 29): ״הבו לה׳ בני אלים הבו לה׳ כבוד ועוז״." "<b>And there are those</b> who explained 'Why do you ask for my name?' — and I am the one who is defeated, and it is the way of the world that the victor reveals his name in order to be honored, but the defeated one does not wish to reveal it, so as not to be shamed." "<b>And he blessed him there.</b> He thanked him for the blessings and agreed to them. And in the Midrash: 'and he blessed him there' — the priestly blessing; ברכו (barekhu)." "<b>And by way of reason (sekhel):</b> 'And a man wrestled with him' — this is Gabriel, and he is the Active Intellect according to the philosophers, and he is the tenth rank among the ranks of the angels called אישים (ishim), and therefore Scripture calls him 'איש (ish).' And Jacob wished to know whether it was possible for his soul to be intellectual while still conjoined with matter, at the level of the Active Intellect that is pure and clean of matter. And he informed him that this is impossible for him until the breaking of dawn — that is, until he departs from the darkness of the body into the bright and pure light whose dwelling is in the dawn." "<b>Here am I.</b> I write for you here the interpretation of the passage in this manner, as a certain philosopher among the sages of our Torah interpreted it — he was from Seville — in the commentary on Song of Songs that he composed. And since that composition is in the language of Ishmael, behold its substitute is holy, for I have translated the language of the commentary into our holy language, and this is its translated wording." "<b>And Jacob was left</b> alone. Jacob was stripped in his thought from the bodily faculties until he remained alone with his rational soul, and he wished to know whether his rational soul would be in the rank of the Active Intellect — which Scripture termed by the word 'man' (איש), and that is Gabriel." "<b>And know</b> that wrestling between two men does not occur unless they are equals, and the intention is to say that Jacob did not have the capacity in this matter to be equal to the Active Intellect until the breaking of dawn — that is, until the intellect departs from the darknesses of the body, which he called 'dawn' (שחר)." "<b>And he saw</b> that he could not prevail — that is, now — for the Active Intellect had no advantage over the rational soul in Jacob at all, except in this matter alone: namely, in his recognizing that the Active Intellect is free from matter, whereas the rational soul that is in Jacob is bound up with matter. This is what it says: <b>and he struck the hollow of his thigh</b> — he likened matter to the hollow of the thigh, upon which the entire body rests. <br><b>And the hollow of Jacob's thigh</b> was strained as he wrestled with him: Scripture indicated that the deficiency of the rank of the rational soul in Jacob relative to the rank of the Active Intellect is on the side of the hollow of Jacob's thigh — that is, on the side of matter." "<b>And he said: Send me, for the dawn has broken.</b> This now alludes to the Active Intellect: for the rational soul that is in Jacob, once it separates from matter — and that is its 'dawn' — will no longer need it at all, for its rank is equal to his rank. And he cited this likening as one who sends off his companion and no longer needs him." "<b>And he said.</b> 'I will not let you go unless you bless me.' Scripture teaches that the rational soul within Jacob takes hold of him and binds itself to him, and therefore he said, 'I will not let you go' — I will not separate from you even for a moment until you bestow influence upon me and return to the rational degree, clean of all matter; therefore your parting from me is impossible until I attain the supernal degree to which I yearn.'" "<b>And he said.</b> 'Your name shall no longer be called Jacob.' Know that the name יעקב (Jacob) indicates lowness, from the language of (Genesis 25:26) ״וידו אוחזת בעקב עשו״ — 'and his hand was holding the heel of Esau' — for the heel is the lowest of all the places in the body, while the name ישראל (Israel) indicates dominion and superiority. And therefore he said: you are worthy to be called by a name that indicates precedence and excellence, not that you should be called by a name indicating lowness and the hindmost. And he explained the reason: <b>for you have struggled with Elohim and with men.</b> He explained that the degree of his rational soul ascended to the degree of the separate intelligibles in the knowledge of intelligibles, even though his soul is combined with matter — this is what is meant by 'and with men,' that is, even though it is combined with the body within the human structure. And since he said 'with Elohim,' there was no need to say 'and with men,' for if he struggled with Elohim, all the more so with men; but the intention is that the degree of Jacob's rational soul is in the degree of Elohim — which are the separate intelligibles — even while it is joined with the body." "<b>Tell me your name, please.</b> He wished to know his own truth — that he would inform him of a name indicating his essence and how the intelligibles are bestowed upon us from Him. But he did not inform him this; he only answered him, 'Why do you ask for my name?' — that is, you will have no need for this question, for you have already attained much and ascended to the degree of the intelligibles. And afterward Scripture says: 'Therefore the children of Israel do not eat the sinew of the thigh-vein' — its meaning is that since the rational soul is worthy of following after the Active Intellect, the disciples of Israel should not engage in matters of physical lusts, which He likened to the sinew of the thigh-vein." "<b>And know</b> that disciples are called 'sons,' and you already know that the sinews of an animal are very strong, like cords and even more than them. And Scripture likened sin to a cord, as it is written (Isaiah 5:18): ״הוי מושכי העון בחבלי השוא וכעבות העגלה חטאה״ — 'Woe to those who draw sin with cords of vanity, and as with cart-ropes their sin' — because although its beginning is weak, its end grows increasingly strong. And through the strength of lusts, HaShem, may He be exalted, is forgotten; and therefore He called it by these two terms: גיד הנשה (the sinew of the thigh-vein). And He attributed eating to it and said 'they shall not eat,' for the pleasantness and delight of the sense of taste is only for the moment." "<b>And from</b> what is well known: moderation in it is good and pleasant, for through it comes the preservation of the body, while excess from it is the destruction and ruin of the body — so too it will be for a person with all the rest of his lusts. 'Which is upon the hollow of the thigh' — because the entire body leans upon it, for all the other lusts enter under the general term of eating. And he explained, and said: 'until this day' — for people conduct themselves in this world with these lusts until they reach the World to Come, which is called 'day'; and he said 'this,' that is, after his departure from this world. 'For he touched the hollow of Jacob's thigh' — that is, for the degree of the rational soul has already ascended to the degree of the intelligibles separate from matter; there is no difference between them except its being combined with matter — this is the meaning of 'for he touched the hollow of Jacob's thigh.' And therefore he mentioned Jacob and attributed the hollow of the thigh to Jacob and not to Israel, because the name יעקב indicates lowness and the pursuit of lowly physical matters, while the name ישראל, the opposite of it, indicates excellence and dominion and the pursuit of intelligibles and knowledge of the separate intelligibles. And Scripture said concerning Elijah (I Kings 18:31): ״ויקח אליהו שתים עשרה אבנים כמספר שבטי בני יעקב אשר היה דבר ה' אליו ישראל יהיה שמך״ — 'And Elijah took twelve stones according to the number of the tribes of the sons of Jacob, to whom the word of YHWH had come, saying, Israel shall be your name.' And what was the need to inform us that YHWH said to him, 'Israel shall be your name,' as if we asked, 'Who is Jacob?' But the matter is that Israel at that time were rebelling against the Holy One, blessed be He, and the prophets of Baal were misleading them. Elijah explained that they were drawn after matter and physical lusts, and all this is indicated by the name יעקב, and his intention in this was that they should be rational, like the ministering angels, as it is written (Psalms 82): ״אני אמרתי אלהים אתם״ — 'I said, you are Elohim,' etc. — but they swerved from the way of HaShem, may He be exalted, and went after the prophets of Baal, who walk in darkness and not light. And therefore Elijah mentioned to them, in their matter, the name יעקב and the name ישראל, to teach them that the ultimate intention is not to be drawn after matter and after physical lusts, but to be drawn and to bind oneself to the degree of the separate intelligibles — just as the ultimate intention was not in the name יעקב but in the name ישראל, for this very reason. And this is what is meant by 'YHWH said to him, Israel shall be your name.' And as the Sages, of blessed memory, said: ישראל is primary and יעקב is subsidiary to it. And from this Scripture said (Exodus 19:3): ״כה תאמר לבית יעקב ותגיד לבני ישראל״ — 'So shall you say to the house of Jacob, and tell the children of Israel.' He attributed the women to יעקב, for they are subsidiary to the men, and he attributed the males to ישראל, for they are the primary. This is the language of the wise philosopher. And if perhaps I did not succeed in copying his language letter by letter, my intention was only to preserve his intention in this path upon which he walks." "<b>And I</b> say, by way of this approach, that it is possible to explain why Scripture varied the naming of the place, once Penuel and once Peniel: for while his thought was ranging over the matter of the angel, he called the place's name Peniel with a yod, alluding to this angel that he is the tenth among the gradations of the angels called אישים; and when he passed that place and was divested of this thought, he returned as at first to the perceptions of this world, which is six directions; therefore he mentioned Penuel with a vav, and this is what it says, ״והוא צולע על ירכו״ — and he was limping upon his thigh — for then he looked into the affairs of the body and felt its sensations." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> ״ויאבק איש עמו״ — a man wrestled with him — this is a created glory among the angels, and this has already been explained in the section of ״וירא אליו ה׳״, and there is no need here to elaborate."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "The Psalms citation is written as תהלים כ״ט (29); the verse 'הבו לה׳ בני אלים' is indeed Psalm 29:1. No discrepancy."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:59.040602409Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}